       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png"
      },
      "displayName": "eddie",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=5fca8c79aca10c0069e36e33",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>This is pretty bad. Without actually looking at the code, I can guess what's happening: the DateUtility method for parsing a datetime string returns null if it can't parse the date. If the caller isn't checking for that and simply passing the returned value the situation described could result.</p>\n\n<p>So, either, check for null return, or, consider actually making the DateUtility method throw an exception (unchecked, maybe illegalargumentexception) but I'm not sure what else that would affect.</p>\n\n<p>For \n    <span class=\"jira-issue-macro resolved\" data-jira-key=\"FCREPO-689\" >\n                <a href=\"https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/browse/FCREPO-689\" class=\"jira-issue-macro-key issue-link\"  title=\"Enable optimistic locking for modify operations\" >\n            <img class=\"icon\" src=\"https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/universal_avatar/view/type/issuetype/avatar/10315?size=medium\" />\n            FCREPO-689\n        </a>\n                                                    <span class=\"aui-lozenge aui-lozenge-subtle aui-lozenge-success jira-macro-single-issue-export-pdf\">Closed</span>\n            </span>\n, I already started some work on parsing datetime strings as rest params and returning 400 errors if there's a date parse error. I only needed it to handle lastModifiedDate, but I can apply it to all the REST params that take datetimes.</p>",
     "created": "24/May/10 7:26 PM",
