       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/64bf6f26854d620d318d4082f2e6a6a7?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FE-3.png"
      },
      "displayName": "eddie",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=5fca8c79aca10c0069e36e33",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>This needs to be verified w/ the SOAP API as well. Glancing at the code, it looks like it suffers from the same problem (see FedoraAPIMBindingSOAPHTTPImpl). </p>\n\n<p>Increasingly thinking that DateUtility should throw at least an unchecked exception if there's a parseexception. However, should a null argument just return null? We have a lot of code that can validly take a null Date (or date string), so either we check for that before calling DateUtility or allow DateUtility to continue to return null for null arguments.</p>",
     "created": "25/May/10 4:42 AM",
